    "type": "reasoning",
    "text": "The CSV is 55KB with 776 lines (775 rows). Reading the whole thing into context is feasible but big. Let me plan: I'll parse the CSV with a script, produce a readable digest of n, text, sop, and then judge. But I as the model need to actually judge each row — 775 rows. That's a lot but doable if I go batch by batch (e.g., 50 rows per read, 16 reads). That's heavy context. \n\nAlternative: delegate to child agents in parallel — but judging requires reading rules carefully too. RULES.md is 16KB. Hmm, but judgments must be consistent.\n\nActually maybe a good approach: I judge in chunks myself. Total CSV 55KB — the whole thing could fit in context (~15k tokens). But then I need to produce 775 verdicts — that's a long output (each ~100 chars → 77KB output). That's a lot of generation. Splitting into parts: write the verdict file in a few large parts as allowed.\n\nLet me consider: reading the entire CSV (55KB) in one go is fine — approximately 14k tokens. Then generating 775 verdicts in, say, 6 parts of ~130 each. Each part ~13KB output. Total output ~80KB. That's a lot but feasible across multiple turns.\n\nActually — should I delegate to subagents? Each subagent would need to read RULES.md too and judge chunks. That risks inconsistency but is much faster. However quality/consistency matters more. Yet 775 rows judged by me in one context — I need to keep the whole CSV in context while writing verdicts, and each part re-reads... Actually the CSV stays in context; I just generate part by part.\n\nHmm, but risk: with 775 rows, my attention degrades. Let me do chunks: read rules (done), then read CSV portion 1 (rows 1-100), judge them immediately and append to file; then next portion. Writing incrementally with append is \"edit line by line\"? No — the instruction says write the whole verdicts file at once, or in a few large parts. So a few large parts, each appended as a big chunk. That's fine: I'll write parts of ~150-200 rows each via bash heredoc or write_file + bash cat.\n\nBut if I process by chunks of CSV reading, the file parts are still large. Fine: 4 chunks of ~200 rows, each write ~150 rows? Let's do: process CSV in chunks of ~100 rows, and accumulate verdict lines in scratchpad, then write file in 4-6 large parts. Simpler: for each CSV chunk of ~100 rows, I generate verdict lines and append them to scratchpad file; then at the end copy scratchpad content to the target file in a few parts. But scratchpad is private; use scratchpad to accumulate? Actually I can just append to the target file with bash heredoc in large parts — each part corresponds to a CSV chunk of 100 rows. That's 8 parts. \"a few large parts\" — 8 is okay-ish. Let me aim for chunks of ~150 rows → 6 parts.\n\nHmm, the real constraint is: don't edit line by line. Appending 150-line chunks is fine.\n\nLet me first extract the CSV in readable form. I'll dump text+sop per row with row numbers. Use python to print each record with sections. Let me check whether the sop field has embedded newlines — yes, quoted multi-line. So python csv needed.\n\nLet me print rows in chunks with compact formatting.\n\nPlan:\n- todo list\n- read rows 1-100 (as text), judge, append.\n\nLet me check for any check.mjs presence in folder — RULES mentions `node check.mjs <k>`. Let me check if it exists in the drawing folder.\n\nLet me look for it.",
