    "text": "Let me judge these 25 first (part of chunk 1).\n\nn=1 \"Make my avatar a man.\" — imperative, verb Make, complements my avatar, a man. Fine. L8.\n\nn=2 declarative, should/be, subject My avatar, complement a male figure. L8.\n\nn=3 \"Set my avatar to the man.\" — imperative verb Set complement n1; relation to from c1 to n2. Fine. L8.\n\nn=4 similar. L8.\n\nn=5 \"Use a guy for my avatar.\" — imperative, complement a guy, relation for c1->my avatar ambiguous. L8.\n\nn=6 \"I'd like a man character for the avatar, please.\" — declarative, 'd auxiliary, like verb, please discourse. Relation for n2->n3. L8.\n\nn=7 \"Choose the male figure for my avatar.\" — complement the male figure, relation for n1->n2. L8.\n\nn=8 \"Pick a man for me.\" — c1 imperative Pick complement a man; for c1->me. L8.\n\nn=9 \"Keep my avatar as a male figure.\" — L8.\n\nn=11 \"The avatar should be a guy.\" — L8.\n\nn=12 \"My preference is the male option.\" — L8.\n\nn=13 \"Let's go with the male figure for the avatar.\" — let verb imperative, 's complement nominal, nonfinite c2 \"go\", relation with from c2 to figure, r3 from c1 to c2 ties. Passes per settled case (let's). L8.\n\nn=16 \"Can you switch my avatar to a guy?\" — interrogative, Can auxiliary, verb switch, subject you, complement my avatar, relation to c1->a guy. L8.\n\nn=17 \"Is there a way to make my avatar the male option?\" — interrogative verb Is, subject there, complement a way; relation to from n2 (a way) to c2 nonfinite make. Fine. L8.\n\nn=18 \"How about a man character for my avatar?\" — form=fragment, modifier How, relation about from c1 to n1. Hmm: \"How about\" — \"about\" marker from fragment clause to nominal. Fragment with modifier How. Acceptable? \"How\" is a question word; settled case: question word stands where grammar puts it — modifier in \"where is it?\" Fine. Relation marked about passing. L8.\n\nn=19 \"Maybe the male figure would be better for my avatar.\" — declarative, modifier Maybe, auxiliary would, verb be, modifier better, subject the male figure, relation for from c1 to my avatar. L8.\n\nn=20 \"I think I'd like a man as my avatar.\" — two clauses, r2 ties. L8.\n\nn=21 \"It would be nice if my avatar were the man.\" — c1 declarative, relation if from c1 to c2. Fine. L8.\n\nn=22 \"I'd rather have a man figure.\" — L8.\n\nn=23 \"Give me a guy for the avatar.\" — L8.\n\nn=25 \"What about a man character for my avatar?\" — c1 fragment complement n1 head What; relation about from c1 to n2. Hmm. \"What\" as head of nominal, complement of fragment. Is this acceptable? \"What about\" - similar to n=18. The settled case about question words: a `head` of a nominal in \"what is this?\". Here \"What\" is a nominal complement of the fragment clause, and \"about\" is a marker linking c1 to \"a man character\". So \"What\" is tied to the clause as complement. Acceptable — the reading is plausible. L8.\n\nn=26 \"When I open a new tab, my avatar should already be the male figure.\" — r1 marker When from c2 to c1: relation starts at c2 (the main clause) and ends at c1. Hmm, per settled case for \"so it sits under the table\": relation marked by clause-end marker from the main clause to the subordinate clause. Here \"When...\" clause precedes; relation starts at c2 (main) and ends at subordinate clause c1. That is consistent with convention (from=clause where relation starts → main clause; to = the marker clause). L8.\n\nn=29 \"I pick a man figure for the avatar.\" L8.\n\nn=30 \"Put the male option as my avatar.\" — complement the male option, relation as c1->my avatar ambiguous. L8.\n\nn=39 \"Choose a man as my avatar and do not change it unless I ask, even after a restart.\" — c1 imperative; coordination and joins c1, c2. c2 imperative with auxiliary do, negation not. Hmm: \"do not change it\" — imperative with auxiliary \"do\" and negation not, verb change. Rules permit auxiliary do? An imperative negative uses \"do not\". Fine. r2 unless from c2 to c3 (declarative ask). \"even\" modifier of c3, r3 after from c3 to a restart. Looks right. Is relation unless from c2 to c3 correct? Yes. L8.\n\nGood, all fine so far. Continue reading next 25.",
