    "type": "reasoning",
    "text": "Judge rows 26-50 (n=42..80).\n\nn=42 \"a guy.\" — fragment, complement a guy. L8.\nn=43 \"the male option, always.\" — fragment modifier always complement the male option. L8.\nn=44 \"a man character, please.\" — fragment discourse please complement. L8.\nn=47 \"Actually, make it a man figure instead.\" — imperative, Actually modifier, verb make, instead modifier. \"Actually\" is discourse-ish but modifier passes (not judged? modifier vs discourse: discourse is \"words said to the listener\"; \"Actually\" is arguably discourse. Is holding it as modifier a failure? L2 is about content words in discourse. Holding a discourse word as modifier isn't a listed failure. Fine. L8.\nn=48 \"No, I meant a guy.\" — discourse No, declarative. L8.\nn=49 \"On second thought, the male option would be better.\" — r1 marker On from c1 to n1 (second thought); c1 declarative would be better subject n2. Marker \"On\" linking clause to \"second thought\". Plausible - \"on second thought\" is a fixed phrase; held as relation with marker \"On\" from clause to nominal. Acceptable. L8.\nn=51 \"Hmm, let's go with the male figure after all, since that is the figure I like best.\" — discourse Hmm; c1 let imperative; c2 nonfinite go with modifiers after/all; r1 with c2->n2; r2 since from c1 to c3; c3 declarative is subject that complement the figure; c4 declarative like best subject I; r4 from n4 (the figure) to c4 — relative clause \"I like best\" tied to \"the figure\". Good, so c4 is tied (L6 avoided). r3 c1->c2. Ok. But wait: \"since that is the figure I like best\" — r2 starts at c1, fine. L8.\nn=57 \"Use the male figure for me, plus add round glasses to the avatar.\" — coordination plus joins c1 and c2. Fine. \"round\" dependent of glasses — plausible (round as adjective). L8.\nn=60 \"Keep my avatar as the man, and put a cap on the avatar.\" — L8.\nn=61 \"Choose a guy for my avatar, then change the avatar's top to blue.\" — c2 complement n4 \"top\", r2 marker \"'s\" from n4 to n3 (the avatar). Hmm: relation \"'s\" ends at n3 and starts at n4. Is the direction right? \"the avatar's top\": \"'s\" marks relation between possessor and possessed. Which direction? Convention probably from the possessed (top) to possessor (avatar)? Uncertain but the words give the tie; direction ambiguous — probably not judged strictly. Also r3 \"to\" from c2 to n5 head \"blue\" — \"to blue\" nominal. Fine. L8.\nn=62..80 all straightforward: L8. n=65 \"I want the female option as my avatar\" — complement n2, relation as c1->n3. Fine.\n\nn=78 \"Could you make my avatar the woman?\" interrogative, subject you, complements. L8.\nn=79 \"Would it be possible to make my avatar a lady?\" — interrogative, Would aux, be verb, possible modifier, subject it, relation to c1->c2. L8.\nn=80 L8.\n\nNow let me write part 1? I planned 50 rows per part → 3 parts. Let's continue reading rows 50-75 first, then write part 1 with 50 verdicts. Actually I've now read 50 rows (indices 0-49 → 50 rows: n=1..80 in the list). Yes rows[:50] read. I'll write part 1 now for those 50.\n\nWait — must double-check word coverage; e.g., in n=51 \"after all\" as modifiers fine.\n\nOne check on n=21: \"It would be nice if my avatar were the man.\" c1 doesn't have complement; fine.\n\nLet me also double check n=43: \"the male option, always.\" — \"always\" is a time word after nominal comma; held as modifier of the clause. Good.\n\nn=42: fragment complement. Good.\n\nNow write part 1 (50 lines) to the verdicts file.\n\nNotes for L8 rows: empty note or one sentence. I'll use \"\" for L8 rows... The spec says note is one sentence; for L8 keep \"\" (as example shows). I'll put \"\" for accepts.\n\nLet me write with python to avoid quoting pain? I can write via write_file (path in cwd) — but I should write in parts. Use write_file for part1 then append parts via bash cat >>. Writing a file with content containing JSON lines — write_file is fine.\n\nActually simpler: build the whole verdicts file at the end with python embedding data? But instruction: work in batch, whole file at once or few large parts. Writing 3 parts of 50 lines each is fine.\n\nI'll use write_file for part 1 with 50 lines.",
