    "text": "Judge rows 51-75 (n=81..130).\n\nn=81 L8. n=82 L8. n=83 L8. n=85 L8. n=86 L8. n=88 L8. n=89 L8. n=92 L8. n=93 L8 (\"in every tab\" relation ambiguous from c1). L8. n=94 L8. n=96 \"Stick with the female figure for my avatar.\" — c1 imperative verb Stick, no complement; r1 with c1->figure. Fine (relation holds the nominal). L8. n=105 L8. n=109,110,111 L8. n=113,114,115,117 L8. n=123 L8. n=126 L8.\n\nn=127 \"I want a lady as my avatar, and add earrings to the avatar.\" — c1 declarative, c2 imperative, coordination and joins c1 and c2. Hmm — can \"and\" join a declarative with an imperative? Per rules: \"A command or a question cannot stand inside another clause, so a coordination that joins an imperative or interrogative clause with such a clause has a wrong conjunct: L3\" — that rule is about coordination joining an imperative with a clause that ends a relation with a marker... Let me reread: \"A clause that ends a relation with a marker ('so it sits under the table', 'if you build two purple blocks') stands inside the clause where the relation starts. A command or a question cannot stand inside another clause, so a coordination that joins an imperative or interrogative clause with such a clause has a wrong conjunct: L3.\"\n\nHere, c2 \"add earrings to the avatar\" does not end a relation with a marker; it's a plain imperative coordinated with a declarative. \"I want a lady as my avatar, and add earrings to the avatar.\" Hmm, coordinating a declarative (statement of want) with an imperative command — grammar-wise, \"and\" can join clauses of different types? That's a coordinate structure; in real usage it's a bit odd but the message does have two clauses joined by \"and\". The rule L3 says a coordination has a wrong conjunct. Is c1 vs c2 the right conjuncts? The message: \"I want a lady as my avatar, and add earrings to the avatar.\" The \"and\" joins the wanting and the adding. That seems right. Judging fairly: acceptable. L8.\n\nn=128,129,130 L8.\n\nContinue reading next 25.",
