    "type": "reasoning",
    "text": "All 150 rows read. All appear acceptable so far. That's suspicious — 100% L8. Let me double-check a few with more scrutiny, particularly ones with unusual constructions:\n\n1. n=18/81/148/214 \"How about a man character for my avatar?\" — fragment with \"How\" as modifier, relation \"about\" from c1 to nominal. Alternatively \"How\" could be part of fragment. Acceptable? Per rules, question words: \"a modifier in 'where is it?'\". Fine.\n\n2. n=25/88/155 \"What about ... ?\" — c1 fragment complement n1 head \"What\"; relation about from c1 to n2. \"What\" nominal held as complement. Fine — settled case about question words allows head of nominal. Though \"what\" as head of a nominal tied as complement of a verbless clause — plausible.\n\n3. n=13/75/143 \"Let's go with the male figure for the avatar.\" — per settled case, \"let\" verb, \"'s\" complement nominal, nonfinite c2 go. Passes.\n\n4. n=21/85/217 \"It would be nice if my avatar were the man.\" — c1 has modifier \"nice\" and no complement; relation if from c1 to c2. Fine.\n\n5. n=26 etc. \"When I open a new tab, ...\" — relation When from c2 to c1: from is main clause. Hmm, requirement 6: \"The end of every relation, its marker, and its start when the words settle it.\" Both ways are plausible conventions here; per the settled case with \"so it sits under the table\" the relation starts at the main clause and ends at the marked clause. Consistent. Accept.\n\n6. n=51/117/184/245 \"Hmm, let's go with the male figure after all, since that is the figure I like best.\" — r4 from n4 to c4 ties relative clause \"I like best\" to \"the figure\". Good, avoids L6.\n\nBut is \"since that is the figure I like best\" — \"that\" nominal subject; \"the figure\" complement; relative clause c4 tied to n4. Fine.\n\n7. n=96/163/227 \"Stick with the female figure for my avatar.\" — c1 verb Stick, no complement; relation with from c1 to nominal; relation for from c1 to my avatar. Fine.\n\n8. n=127/253 \"I want a lady as my avatar, and add earrings...\" — coordination joins declarative c1 with imperative c2. Is that a \"wrong conjunct\"? The rules' L3 case is specific: a coordination joining an imperative with a clause that stands inside another clause. Here both are main clauses. Accept.\n\n9. n=19/82 \"Maybe the male figure would be better for my avatar.\" — \"Maybe\" modifier of the clause; fine (could be discourse, both pass? It's not judged).\n\n10. n=23 \"Give me a guy for the avatar.\" — complement me, complement a guy; relation for from n2 to n3. Fine.\n\n11. n=1: \"Make my avatar a man.\" — two complements, no relation. Fine.\n\n12. n=47: \"Actually, make it a man figure instead.\" — \"Actually\" as modifier — could be discourse; not a content word so no L2. Fine.\n\n13. n=49/115/182/243 \"On second thought, the male option would be better.\" — relation marker \"On\" from c1 to n1 (\"second thought\"). \"On\" is a preposition, linking word. Fine.\n\n14. n=43/110/177/238 \"the male option, always.\" — modifier always in fragment. Fine.\n\n15. n=93/225 \"Use a woman figure as my avatar in every tab.\" — relations as and in from c1. Fine.\n\n16. n=63: fine.\n\n17. n=129 \"My avatar should be an older man.\" — \"older\" dependent. Fine.\n\n18. n=17/147/213 \"Is there a way to make my avatar the male option?\" — c1 verb Is subject there complement a way; relation to from n2 to c2. Fine.\n\n19. n=79/145/211 \"Would it be possible to make my avatar a lady?\" — modifier \"possible\", relation to from c1. Fine.\n\n20. n=105/39 — auxiliary \"do\" with negation \"not\"; per rules auxiliary words must be right. \"do\" is auxiliary in \"do not change\". Fine.\n\nEverything is L8. Is that plausible? The dataset is \"objects-01-simple\" with label_state=agreement — meaning spaCy and the rules-based formalizer agree; these are the \"simple\" rows where two independent systems agreed. So few errors expected. But the prompt expects some rejections maybe. Let me be more skeptical on specific candidate rows where a mistake might hide:\n\nRe-examine n=6 \"I'd like a man character for the avatar, please.\" — n1 head \"I\" no dependents. Fine.\n\nn=61 \"Choose a guy for my avatar, then change the avatar's top to blue.\" — c2 complement n4 \"top\" and relation 's from n4 to n3; relation to from c2 to n5 \"blue\" (head blue, nominal). \"blue\" as a nominal with head blue; per rules \"change the top to blue\" — \"to blue\" nominal. Fine. Hmm, but \"blue\" is an adjective; a nominal with head \"blue\" — acceptable reading (a color name). Not judged.\n\nn=1 check: \"Make my avatar a man.\" — no relation; ok.\n\nn=39: \"do not change it unless I ask, even after a restart.\" — r3 marker after from c3 (I ask) to n5 (a restart). \"even\" modifier of c3. Fine.\n\nAlso potential L6: any clause or nominal tied to nothing? In n=20, n1 \"I\" tied as subject; n2 subject; ok. In n=61 n3/n4 tied. In n=13 all tied.\n\nCheck n=25: n1 \"What\" is complement of c1; r1 from c1 to n2. Fine.\n\nCheck n=183: \"Change that to an elderly gentleman, please.\" — discourse please. Fine.\n\nSo verdicts: all L8. Hmm. Let me consider whether some rows are actually questionable enough to reject under a \"fair but not rubber-stamp\" standard. The rules emphasize: a plausible reading the next layer can continue from is acceptable. Given the dataset was built from agreement between two systems and these messages are simple avatar-setting requests, all L8 is plausible.\n\nBut wait — let me double check some rows for word-coverage errors that indicate a lost word (which might show as a clause missing a verb, e.g., a verb held as modifier). Let me programmatically check that no row has a multi-word text with no verb at all where a verb exists. Specifically flag rows where the sop has form=fragment but the text contains a likely verb. From reading, fragments are: n=42,43,44,109,110,111,176,177,178,237,238,151,216 (\"Perhaps an older man for the avatar\") — all verbless messages. Good.\n\nAlso flag rows where a clause has no verb key at all — none.\n\nOne more check: does every row's clause have verb when the text has a verb? I read all rows and each sop looked structurally consistent.\n\nLet me do a mechanical check for suspicious items: rows where a text word is missing from the sop (word coverage). The problem statement said a program already checked coverage, but let's verify to catch lost info. I'll write a quick script comparing token sets ignoring punctuation.\n\nThat's a good sanity check for L5/L6-type issues and for typos in my reading.",
