    "type": "reasoning",
    "text": "The 20 mismatches are all contraction splitting artifacts (\"I'd\" → I + 'd, \"Let's\" → let + 's, \"avatar's\" → avatar + 's). No real coverage loss. Good — my reading was accurate.\n\nSo all 150 rows are L8. Hmm, but \"judge fairly: plausible or incomplete reading acceptable; reject only a wrong statement or lost explicit information\". All rows genuinely satisfy the rules as far as I checked. But let me be extra careful on a couple of subtle rule areas that this data intersects:\n\nA) \"How about X?\" — Is a fragment acceptable, or should it be interrogative? The message is arguably a question (\"How about a woman character for my avatar?\"). Rules: L1 if \"a clear question written as imperative or declarative, or the reverse\". A fragment is neither imperative nor declarative exactly; and there's no verb, so fragment is right (a message with no verb is a fragment per settled case: \"a message or a part with no verb ('pie radius smaller', 'legend on the right', 'a fireplace') is a fragment\"). \"How about X\" has no verb. So fragment is correct. Good.\n\nB) n=26 etc.: \"When I open a new tab, my avatar should already be the male figure.\" Both clauses have subjects/aux. Fine.\n\nC) n=39: coordination \"and\" conjunct c1, c2 — but c2 begins with relation r2 (unless...) which is a relation from c2 to c3, not a clause that ends a relation with a marker standing inside another clause. Wait, careful: the settled case says \"A clause that ends a relation with a marker ('so it sits under the table', 'if you build two purple blocks') stands inside the clause where the relation starts.\" In n=39, c3 \"I ask\" ends the relation marked \"unless\"? No — r2 is marker \"unless\" from c2 to c3, so c3 ends the relation. Is c3 inside c2? Yes, c3 stands inside c2. That's fine, no coordination joins c3 with a command. Coordination k1 joins c1 and c2 — both imperatives at top level. Fine.\n\nD) In n=51 pattern, r2 \"since\" from c1 to c3, c3 contains c4 tied via r4. Good.\n\nE) n=127: \"I want a lady as my avatar, and add earrings to the avatar.\" Coordination joins c1 (declarative) with c2 (imperative). Fine.\n\nNow, is 150/150 L8 a defensible outcome? Given the CSV was pre-filtered to cases where two independent systems agreed (label_state=agreement), yes. Still, let me spot-check a handful against a stricter eye on requirement 5 (subject) and requirement 3 (ellipsis). \n\nOne thing I want to double check: n=2 \"My avatar should be a male figure.\" — auxiliary should, verb be. Good.\n\nn=96 \"Stick with the female figure for my avatar.\" — Verb \"Stick\" is imperative; the prepositional \"with ...\" is the complement via relation. Good.\n\nAlso n=5 \"Use a guy for my avatar.\" — complement a guy. Good.\n\nLet me check whether any row has a redundant/duplicate problem like two identical complement references, e.g., n=78 \"complement=$n2 complement=$n3\" — fine.\n\nCheck for any row where the same wire is both subject and complement, or where subject is held on a clause whose subject is a nominal also complement of another clause — not seen.\n\nI'm fairly confident. Let me also double check n=16 \"Can you switch my avatar to a guy?\" - relation to from c1 to a guy; but \"you\" is subject; fine.\n\nAnother check: rows where a clause is missing that the message has. E.g., n=17: \"Is there a way to make my avatar the male option?\" — the nonfinite clause c2 has verb \"make\" and complements; good, no L5.\n\nRow n=145: \"to make my avatar an older gentleman\" — c2 nonfinite with complements; fine.\n\nTime to also check ones with two-way relations potentially wrong: n=7 \"Choose the male figure for my avatar.\" relation from n1 (the figure) to n2 (my avatar). Fine.\n\nn=29 \"I pick a man figure for the avatar.\" relation for from n2 to n3. Fine.\n\nn=67 same. n=201 relation for from n1 to n2. Fine.\n\nOK. Now write part 3 with the remaining 50 rows: rows 100-149 indexes → n=176..257 listed:\n\nFrom the third read (indices 100-124): 176,177,178,181,182,183,184,189,190,193,194,195,196,197,198,199,200,201,202,203,206,210,211,212,213 — 25 rows.\nFrom the fourth read (indices 125-149): 214,215,216,217,218,219,221,225,226,227,237,238,241,242,243,244,245,248,249,252,253,254,255,256,257 — 25 rows.\n\nTotal 150. Good.\n\nWrite part 3.\n\nWait — before that, should I reconsider a couple of rows more critically? Let me reconsider n=93/225: \"Use a woman figure as my avatar in every tab.\" — relation \"in\" from c1 to \"every tab\" with attachment ambiguous. Fine.\n\nn=183: \"Change that to an elderly gentleman, please.\" — discourse please at clause level. Fine.\n\nn=151/216: \"Perhaps an older man for the avatar.\" — fragment with modifier \"Perhaps\" and complement. Fine.\n\nn=241: \"Actually, make it an older lady instead.\" — fine.\n\nAlso n=131 etc fine.\n\nLet me now write the remaining 50 lines and verify the whole file.\n\nAlso: grading distribution — is there any row I judge as L8 but where a rule genuinely flags something? Let me revisit two candidates more deeply: \n\nn=49: \"On second thought, the male option would be better.\" — the relation r1: marker \"On\", from c1 to n1. Alternative: \"On second thought\" as fixed phrase modifier. Both pass? Rules for fixed phrases of time/manner allow holding whole as modifier. Here they used a relation; that's another reading, doesn't state anything wrong. Accept.\n\nn=51: c2 \"go\" has modifiers \"after\" and \"all\" — \"after all\" as two modifier words; the settled case allows a fixed phrase held whole as modifier (as one modifier value or presumably split?). The rule says \"A fixed phrase of time or manner ('from now on', 'after that', 'for now', 'side by side', 'at once') may be held whole as a `modifier`.\" Held as two separate modifier words — is that \"split\" causing an error? It's still in the modifier field of the same clause, so no information lost and nothing wrong stated. Also \"after\" alone could be read as a preposition — but here it's held as modifier, not a marker, so no wrong relation. Accept.\n\nn=26: relation r1 marker \"When\" from c2 to c1. Is \"When\" a linking word? Yes, subordinating conjunction. Fine.\n\nn=39: \"unless\" from c2 to c3 — correct.\n\nGood. Write part 3 now.",
