    "text": "The file is complete and valid: 150 lines, one per row, in CSV order. Only my file is new in this folder (other untracked entries pre-existed from other work, not mine — I only created objects-01-simple.p01.verdicts.jsonl).\n\nUpdate todos and report.\n\nAll 150 rows got L8. Should I mention my skepticism? Yes — state the reasoning: the packet is drawn from rows where two formalizers agreed, and every circuit I read matched the words' clause split, verbs, nominals and ties. Also mention deliberate judgement calls I accepted (neighboring readings) and that I flagged none.\n\nWait — one concern: 100% accept on a judged sample could be seen as rubber stamping. Let me do one more careful pass on a random subset with fresh eyes, to be honest about the \"judge fairly but don't rubber-stamp\" balance. Let me re-examine ~15 rows I read quickly, comparing sop to text word by word, searching for a subtle wrong statement (wrong head, subject/complement swap).\n\nCandidates with potential swap risk: n=2 \"My avatar should be a male figure.\" subject My avatar, complement a male figure — right. n=12 \"My preference is the male option.\" right. n=19 \"Maybe the male figure would be better for my avatar.\" subject the male figure, \"better\" modifier — right. n=65 \"I want the female option as my avatar.\" subject I, complement the female option; relation as c1->my avatar — right. n=133 \"I want an old man figure as my avatar.\" — complement n2, relation as from n2 to n3 — fine. n=199 \"I want an elderly lady as my avatar.\" relation as from c1 to n3 — fine (ambiguous attachment allowed).\n\nn=17 \"Is there a way to make my avatar the male option?\" — \"there\" subject, \"a way\" complement. Right.\n\nn=79 \"Would it be possible to make my avatar a lady?\" — subject it; \"possible\" modifier; right.\n\nHead checks: \"man character\" head character; \"woman figure\" head figure; \"old man\" head man; \"older gentleman\" head gentleman; \"elderly lady\" head lady; \"the